  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.5 z²) / 2, drawn at 0.4 × loss over x, z in [-2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first; plain steps diverge along x once the learning rate is above 0.8.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise per component has spread 1.5 × √((64 − b)/(63 b)), like sampling b of 64 points, and is exactly zero at b = 64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at 2 × the true step length. Orange: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Grey: −learning rate × true slope (full data). The step-length readout is the true length, 0 once the run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). Off the bowl the ball is drawn at its edge; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed sequence: Reset replays it from the start, and a new run carries on with the next values. Each trail holds the start plus up to 40 steps.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average; small batches can raise it on single steps."
  ]
}